<% add_breadcrumb "Docs", nil, class: "active" %>

<div class="max-w-3xl">
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">Admin Guide</h1>

  <%# Table of contents %>
  <nav class="mb-10 bg-white shadow sm:rounded-md px-6 py-4">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3">Contents</p>
    <ol class="space-y-1 text-sm text-blue-600 list-decimal list-inside">
      <li><a href="#project-structure" class="hover:underline">Project Structure</a></li>
      <li><a href="#development-server" class="hover:underline">Development Server</a></li>
      <li><a href="#backups" class="hover:underline">Backups</a></li>
      <li><a href="#error-monitoring" class="hover:underline">Error Monitoring (Bugsnag)</a></li>
      <li><a href="#admin-accounts" class="hover:underline">Admin Accounts</a></li>
      <li><a href="#permission-levels" class="hover:underline">Permission Levels</a></li>
      <li><a href="#managing-areas" class="hover:underline">Managing Areas</a></li>
      <li><a href="#bulk-upload" class="hover:underline">Bulk Upload (Problems)</a></li>
      <li><a href="#photo-import" class="hover:underline">Importing Problems from Photos</a></li>
      <li><a href="#location-editor" class="hover:underline">Location Editor (Drag-and-Drop)</a></li>
      <li><a href="#adding-boulders" class="hover:underline">Adding Boulders (GeoJSON)</a></li>
      <li><a href="#boulder-editor" class="hover:underline">Boulder Editor (In-Browser)</a></li>
      <li><a href="#geojson-import" class="hover:underline">GeoJSON Import Workflow</a></li>
      <li><a href="#problem-editing" class="hover:underline">Individual Problem Editing</a></li>
      <li><a href="#problem-description" class="hover:underline">Problem Description</a></li>
      <li><a href="#topos" class="hover:underline">Topos and Line Drawing</a></li>
      <li><a href="#circuits" class="hover:underline">Circuits</a></li>
      <li><a href="#pois" class="hover:underline">POIs and Routes</a></li>
      <li><a href="#contributions" class="hover:underline">Contributions</a></li>
      <li><a href="#offline-use" class="hover:underline">Offline Use (Map &amp; Photos)</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <%# Sections %>
  <div class="space-y-10">

    <%# Project Structure %>
    <section id="project-structure">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Project Structure" %>
      <div class="prose-content">
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4 overflow-x-auto leading-relaxed">app/
  controllers/
    admin/                   # All admin controllers (require authentication)
    map_data_controller.rb   # Serves problems + boulders as GeoJSON for the map
    area_labels_controller.rb# Serves area name labels as GeoJSON for the map
  models/
    area.rb                  # Climbing area (has many problems, boulders, circuits)
    problem.rb               # An individual climbing problem
    boulder.rb               # A physical boulder (polygon geometry)
    circuit.rb               # A colour-coded circuit grouping problems
    topo.rb                  # A photo with line drawings on it
    line.rb                  # A drawn line on a topo linking it to a problem
    poi.rb                   # Point of Interest (parking, train station)
    poi_route.rb             # Transport route from a POI to an area
  views/
    admin/                   # Admin HTML views
    map/                     # Public-facing map view
  javascript/controllers/
    mapbox_controller.js     # All map behaviour (layers, filters, popups)
db/
  schema.rb                  # Database schema
docs/
  admin_guide.md             # Offline copy of this guide</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Development Server %>
    <section id="development-server">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Development Server" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>The development server runs as two systemd user services that start automatically at boot.</p>

        <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 border border-gray-200 rounded-md overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Service</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Purpose</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-100 text-xs">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">boolder-rails</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Puma web server on port 3000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">boolder-css</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Tailwind CSS watcher — recompiles on file changes</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Managing the services</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4 overflow-x-auto"># Status
systemctl --user status boolder-rails boolder-css

# Stop / start / restart
systemctl --user stop boolder-rails boolder-css
systemctl --user start boolder-rails boolder-css
systemctl --user restart boolder-rails

# Follow logs
journalctl --user -u boolder-rails -f
journalctl --user -u boolder-css -f</pre>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Service files</p>
        <p>Located at <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">~/.config/systemd/user/</code>. Environment variables are loaded from the project's <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">.env</code> file. Boot auto-start requires <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">loginctl enable-linger ed</code> (already configured).</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Backups %>
    <section id="backups">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Backups" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Daily automated backups run via a systemd timer and rsync to hosted webspace at <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">edsouthwood.com</code>.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">What is backed up</p>
        <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 border border-gray-200 rounded-md overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Item</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Webspace location</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-100 text-xs">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">PostgreSQL database</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">bowda-backup/db/</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Compressed pg_dump, timestamped, 7-day rolling retention</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">Uploaded files</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">bowda-backup/storage/</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Incremental rsync — current state only</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">config/master.key</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">bowda-backup/master.key</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Required to decrypt credentials</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">.env</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">bowda-backup/.env</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Mapbox key, admin credentials</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Managing backups</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4 overflow-x-auto"># Run a backup immediately
bin/backup

# Check backup logs
tail -f log/backup.log

# Check timer status
systemctl --user status boolder-backup.timer</pre>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Recovery</p>
        <p>To restore after a machine failure:</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li>Clone the repo from GitHub</li>
          <li>Restore <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">.env</code> and <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">config/master.key</code> from the webspace</li>
          <li>Restore the database: <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">gunzip -c dartmoor-dev-YYYYMMDD.sql.gz | psql dartmoor-dev</code></li>
          <li>Restore storage files: <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">rsync -az u79222@edsouthwood.com:bowda-backup/storage/ storage/</code></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Error Monitoring %>
    <section id="error-monitoring">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Error Monitoring (Bugsnag)" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Production errors are reported to <a href="https://app.bugsnag.com" class="text-emerald-700 underline">Bugsnag</a>, tagged with the deployed git commit.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li><strong>Browser</strong> (JavaScript errors only; nothing is sent unless an error happens): <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">app/views/layouts/_bugsnag.html.erb</code>, in every layout. Its API key is public by design. Offline pages skip it.</li>
          <li><strong>Server</strong> (Rails exceptions and background jobs): <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">config/initializers/bugsnag.rb</code>, using <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">bugsnag: api_key</code> from the production credentials. Without it, startup logs "No valid API key has been set".</li>
          <li>Visitor IP addresses are kept out of both, and nothing is stored on visitors' devices. The privacy policy describes this — update it if the setup changes.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Admin Accounts %>
    <section id="admin-accounts">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Admin Accounts" %>
      <div class="prose-content space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
        <p>Credentials are stored in Rails encrypted credentials. Edit them with:</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4">rails credentials:edit</pre>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Legacy format (treated as super admin)</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4">admin_accounts:
  yourusername: "yourpassword"</pre>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">New format with roles</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4">admin_accounts:
  # Super admin — full access
  alice:
    password: "somepassword"
    role: super_admin

  # Area admin — restricted to listed areas
  bob:
    password: "otherpassword"
    role: area_admin
    areas:
      - bonehill
      - haytor</pre>

        <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Plain string credentials continue to work unchanged and are treated as super_admin.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Permission Levels %>
    <section id="permission-levels">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Permission Levels" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 border border-gray-200 rounded-md overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-gray-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Role</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Access</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-100">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-purple-700">super_admin</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2">Full access — create/delete areas, all imports, bulk uploads, POIs, audits</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-blue-700">area_admin</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2">Edit assigned areas and their problems, topos, lines; download/upload GeoJSON for assigned areas; view contributions</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li>Area creation and deletion — super admin only</li>
          <li>Circuits — super admin only</li>
          <li>Bulk uploads, POIs, audit log — super admin only</li>
          <li>GeoJSON import apply step — scoped to your assigned areas</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Managing Areas %>
    <section id="managing-areas">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Managing Areas" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Areas are the top-level groupings. Each has problems, boulders, circuits, and POI routes.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Creating an area <span class="text-xs font-normal text-gray-400">(super admin only)</span></p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Go to <strong>Admin → Areas → New area</strong></li>
          <li>Fill in <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">name</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">slug</code> (URL-friendly, e.g. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">bonehill-rocks</code>), descriptions</li>
          <li>Leave <strong>published</strong> unchecked until the area is ready</li>
          <li>Add problems and boulders before publishing</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Tags</p>
        <p>Available: <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">popular</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">beginner_friendly</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">family_friendly</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">dry_fast</code></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Bulk Upload %>
    <section id="bulk-upload">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Bulk Upload (Problems)" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>The fastest way to add many problems to an area. Go to <strong>Admin → Bulk Uploads</strong>.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">CSV format</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4 overflow-x-auto">name,grade,steepness,sit_start,lat,lon,image_filename,ukc_url,description
Overhanging Scoop,5c,overhang,false,50.5712,-3.9341,scoop.jpg,,Classic line up the right side.
Low Traverse,4a,wall,false,50.5715,-3.9345,,,
Unnamed Slab,,slab,false,,,,</pre>

        <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 border border-gray-200 rounded-md overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Column</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Required</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-100 text-xs">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">name</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Can be blank for unnamed problems</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">grade</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Font grade: 4, 5, 5+, 6a, 6b+, 7a, etc.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">steepness</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold text-red-600">Yes</td><td class="px-4 py-2">See valid values below</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">sit_start</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="px-4 py-2"><code>true</code> or leave blank</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">lat / lon</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Decimal degrees, e.g. 50.5712 / -3.9341</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">image_filename</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Must match uploaded image filename exactly</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">ukc_url</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Full UKC URL for the problem</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">description</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Free-text description shown on the problem page</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Valid steepness values</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded"><%= Problem::STEEPNESS_VALUES.join(", ") %></p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Uploading images</p>
        <p>Upload image files alongside the CSV. Each image creates a topo — draw line coordinates on it afterwards in the topo editor.</p>

        <%= link_to "Go to Bulk Upload →", new_admin_bulk_upload_path, class: "inline-block mt-2 text-blue-600 hover:underline text-sm" %>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Photo Import %>
    <section id="photo-import">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Importing Problems from Photos" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>If you have a folder of photos named with problem names and grades, import them all with one command.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Filename convention</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4">Name_With_Underscores-grade.JPG

# Examples:
American_Squeeze_Job-7b+.JPG  →  "American Squeeze Job"  7b+
Walla_slab-5.JPG              →  "Walla slab"  5
The_Blimp-5+.JPG              →  "The Blimp"  5+</pre>

        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li>Use underscores for spaces in names</li>
          <li>Separate name and grade with a hyphen — the <strong>last</strong> hyphen is the separator</li>
          <li>Photos must be JPEGs with GPS coordinates in the EXIF (phone camera is fine)</li>
          <li>The folder name must match the area's slug or name (case-insensitive)</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Running the import</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4"># Folder inside the project root
rails import:problems FOLDER=bearacleave

# Absolute path
rails import:problems FOLDER=/path/to/haytor</pre>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">What gets created per photo</p>
        <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 border border-gray-200 rounded-md overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Record</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Values</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-100 text-xs">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">Problem</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Name, grade, steepness: wall, lat/lon from photo GPS EXIF</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">Topo</td><td class="px-4 py-2">The photo, attached via ActiveStorage</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">Line</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Links topo to problem — no coordinates yet (draw manually in admin)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="bg-yellow-50 border border-yellow-200 rounded-md p-3 text-xs text-yellow-800">
          <strong>Note:</strong> Running the task twice on the same folder creates duplicates. Run once per folder. The area must already exist in admin before importing.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Location Editor %>
    <section id="location-editor">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Location Editor (Drag-and-Drop)" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Set GPS coordinates for unlocated problems directly in the browser — no GeoJSON export needed.</p>
        <p>Go to <strong>Admin → Areas → [area] → dot menu → Location editor</strong>.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">How to use</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
          <li>The <strong>left sidebar</strong> lists all problems without a location, sorted alphabetically</li>
          <li>The <strong>map</strong> shows boulder polygons for context and green pins for already-located problems</li>
          <li><strong>Drag</strong> a problem name from the sidebar and <strong>drop</strong> it on the map at the correct position — saved instantly</li>
          <li>To <strong>reposition</strong> an already-placed problem, drag its green pin to a new spot — saves on release</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="bg-yellow-50 border border-yellow-200 rounded-md p-3 text-xs text-yellow-800">
          <strong>Bad coordinates?</strong> If problems were uploaded with wrong coordinates (e.g. all at the same point), clear them first:
          <pre class="mt-1 bg-yellow-100 rounded p-2 overflow-x-auto">bundle exec rails runner "Area.find_by(slug: 'area-slug').problems.update_all(location: nil)"</pre>
          Then use the location editor to place them correctly.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Adding Boulders %>
    <section id="adding-boulders">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Adding Boulders" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Boulders are grey polygon outlines shown on the map at zoom 16+. Each <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">Boulder</code> record stores a PostGIS polygon.</p>

        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-3">
          <li>
            <strong>Download the area's GeoJSON</strong> from the area edit page. This file contains existing problem points and boulder polygons.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Open in <a href="https://geojson.io" target="_blank" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">geojson.io</a></strong> — drag and drop the file. Switch to <strong>Satellite</strong> base layer.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Draw polygons</strong> around each boulder using the polygon tool (pentagon icon). No properties are needed — just the shape.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Export as GeoJSON</strong>, then upload via <strong>Admin → Imports → New</strong>. Preview the diff and click Apply.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-md p-3 text-xs text-blue-800">
          <strong>Tip:</strong> OpenStreetMap may already have boulder polygons for well-mapped areas. Export via
          <a href="https://overpass-turbo.eu" target="_blank" class="underline">Overpass Turbo</a>
          using the query <code>natural=rock</code>.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Boulder Editor %>
    <section id="boulder-editor">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Boulder Editor (In-Browser)" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Trace boulder outlines directly in the browser over satellite imagery. No GeoJSON export needed.</p>
        <p>Go to <strong>Admin → Areas → [area] → dot menu → Boulder editor</strong>.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Drawing a new boulder</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Click <strong>Draw boulder</strong> — cursor changes to a crosshair</li>
          <li>Click on the map to place each vertex around the boulder outline</li>
          <li><strong>Double-click</strong> to finish and save — the polygon appears immediately</li>
          <li>Press <strong>Escape</strong> to cancel</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Editing an existing boulder</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Click any grey polygon on the map to select it (turns blue)</li>
          <li>Drag the blue vertex dots to reshape the outline</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Save changes</strong> to save, or click away to discard</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Deleting a boulder</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Click the polygon to select it</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Delete boulder</strong> and confirm</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">LiDAR hillshade overlay</p>
        <p>Under <strong>LiDAR hillshade</strong> in the sidebar, click <strong>LiDAR off</strong> to switch it on. Environment Agency 1&nbsp;m LiDAR is laid over the satellite image as shaded relief, so boulders show as crisp bumps with no shadows or lichen. Use the slider to blend it with the satellite image. Your choice is remembered in this browser.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>1&nbsp;m resolution: use it to find boulders and judge edges, and trace with the satellite visible underneath</li>
          <li>Satellite imagery can be a few metres out of place; where they disagree, trust the LiDAR</li>
          <li>Trees and gorse also show as bumps, but look softer and lumpier than rock</li>
          <li>Served live by the Environment Agency (England only). If it doesn't load, their service is down</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">LiDAR boulder suggestions</p>
        <p>Click <strong>Suggest boulders in this view</strong>. The editor analyses the Environment Agency's LiDAR heights for the current view and outlines anything that looks like rock: <strong>amber</strong> where nothing is mapped, <strong>green</strong> where it overlaps a mapped boulder (the LiDAR is often the more accurate outline).</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Click a suggestion for its size and height, then <strong>Add as new boulder</strong> (saved and selected for tidying) or <strong>Replace outline of #N</strong> (not saved until you click <strong>Save changes</strong>)</li>
          <li>A selected boulder with an overlapping suggestion shows a <strong>Use LiDAR outline</strong> button</li>
          <li>Rock = over 1&nbsp;m above ground where the first and last laser returns agree, so gorse and trees are ignored; shapes under 3&nbsp;m² are left out</li>
          <li>Touching boulders often merge — you'll be warned when a suggestion covers several boulders; trim it before saving</li>
          <li>Outlines are good to about ±0.5&nbsp;m; boulders under 1&nbsp;m high are missed; walls and parked cars can appear</li>
          <li>Works on views under about 750&nbsp;m across. Click <strong>Suggest again</strong> after panning or editing</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-md p-3 text-xs text-blue-800">
          <strong>Tip:</strong> Zoom in to level 19 or higher before tracing — satellite detail is much clearer at high zoom. Click a boulder ID in the sidebar to fly the map to it. On a phone the sidebar sits above the map — scroll it to reach Basemap and LiDAR.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# GeoJSON Import %>
    <section id="geojson-import">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "GeoJSON Import Workflow" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Used to update problem locations and boulder polygons in bulk. Go to <strong>Admin → Imports → New</strong>.</p>

        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
          <li>Upload a <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">.geojson</code> file (must be a FeatureCollection)</li>
          <li>Preview page shows exactly what will be added or changed</li>
          <li>If there are conflicts (records edited since the file was exported) the import is blocked</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Apply</strong> to commit all changes</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">File rules</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li>All features must belong to the same area</li>
          <li><strong>Point</strong> features with a <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">problemId</code> property → update problem locations</li>
          <li><strong>Polygon</strong> features → update or create boulder outlines</li>
          <li>New polygons (no <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">boulderId</code>) create new boulder records</li>
        </ul>

        <%= link_to "Go to Imports →", admin_imports_path, class: "inline-block mt-2 text-blue-600 hover:underline text-sm" %>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Problem Editing %>
    <section id="problem-editing">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Individual Problem Editing" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Navigate to a problem via <strong>Admin → Areas → [area] → [circuit]</strong> then click a problem.</p>
        <p>From the problem show page you can:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li>Edit name, grade, steepness, sit start, UKC URL</li>
          <li>Set or update location (lat/lon)</li>
          <li>Add a topo photo or draw lines on existing topos</li>
          <li>View and respond to contribution requests</li>
          <li>Link to a circuit via the edit form</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Problem Description %>
    <section id="problem-description">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Problem Description" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Each problem has an optional free-text description field for beta, key moves, starting instructions, or any other notes visible to users on the public problem page.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Adding or editing a description</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Open a problem in admin and click <strong>Edit</strong></li>
          <li>Fill in the <strong>Description</strong> field at the bottom of the form</li>
          <li>Save — the text will appear below the topo photo on the public problem page</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Leave the field blank and nothing will be shown. Plain text only — no markdown or HTML.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Topos %>
    <section id="topos">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Topos and Line Drawing" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Topos are photos with line drawings that show where to climb on a boulder face.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Uploading a topo</p>
        <p>Go to <strong>Admin → Topos → New</strong>. Upload the photo. Optionally upload a metadata JSON file (from the iOS app) to auto-link to problems.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Drawing lines</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Open a problem's show page</li>
          <li>Click <strong>New line</strong> or edit an existing one</li>
          <li>Click on the photo to place control points</li>
          <li>Save — the line links the topo to the problem</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Deleting a topo</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Find the topo ID from the problem's show page</li>
          <li>Navigate to <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">/en/admin/topos/&lt;ID&gt;</code></li>
          <li>Click the red <strong>Delete topo</strong> button and confirm</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">Deleting a topo also permanently deletes all its associated lines.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Circuits %>
    <section id="circuits">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Circuits" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Circuits group problems by colour and difficulty. Each problem can belong to one circuit.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li>Circuit details (colour, risk) — super admin only</li>
          <li>Circuit membership is set per-problem via the problem edit form</li>
          <li>Circuits filter the map and appear on area pages</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# POIs %>
    <section id="pois">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "POIs and Routes" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Points of Interest (parking, train stations) help users navigate to areas.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li><strong>Admin → POIs</strong> — manage POI records (super admin only)</li>
          <li><strong>Admin → POI Routes</strong> — link POIs to areas with distance and transport type</li>
          <li>POI routes appear on the area edit page and in the mobile app</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Contributions %>
    <section id="contributions">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Contributions" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Users submit photos and route information via the public contribution form. The form includes:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li><strong>Boulder photo</strong> — GPS coordinates auto-extracted from photo EXIF where available</li>
          <li><strong>Route line</strong> — drawn on the uploaded photo using an in-browser canvas tool. Contributors can also select an existing topo from the area and draw on that instead of uploading a new photo.</li>
          <li><strong>UKC link</strong> — optional link to the problem on UK Climbing</li>
          <li><strong>GPS location</strong> — auto-populated from photo, or entered manually</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The location is set by dragging a pin on a map (it stays in sync with the lat/lon fields and the photo's EXIF GPS). The public form is rate-limited and honeypot-protected against spam, and rejects empty submissions.</p>
        <p>Go to <strong>Admin → Contributions</strong> to review submissions. The list opens on the <strong>pending</strong> queue, with photo thumbnails, dates and comment previews for quick triage, a pending-count badge, and pagination. The review page shows the boulder photo with any drawn route line overlaid, the UKC link, GPS coordinates, and contributor comments.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li><strong>pending</strong> — awaiting review</li>
          <li><strong>accepted</strong> — accepted and applied to the data</li>
          <li><strong>closed</strong> — declined</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-600">Contributors who left an email are notified automatically on submit, acceptance and decline. When declining, fill in the <strong>Note to contributor</strong> field to tell them why — it's included in the decline email. Staff alerts for new contributions go to the addresses in the <code>CONTRIBUTION_EMAILS</code> env var (comma-separated).</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Partial acceptance</p>
        <p>When setting state to <strong>accepted</strong>, checkboxes control what gets applied. Their labels adapt to what the contribution contains:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li><strong>Line on Topo #N</strong> — the contributor drew on an existing topo; accepting only adds the line, no new photo is created</li>
          <li><strong>Photo &amp; line (new topo)</strong> — the contributor uploaded a photo and drew a line; accepting creates a new topo with the line overlay</li>
          <li><strong>Photo (new topo, no line drawn)</strong> — photo uploaded without a line</li>
          <li><strong>GPS coordinates</strong> — copies the contributor's location to the problem (only if the problem has no existing location; a hint shows if it's already located)</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Checkboxes are checked by default; if the contribution has no photo/line or no GPS, a "nothing to import" note shows instead. Uncheck either to skip that part — useful when the photo is good but the GPS is inaccurate, or vice versa. Accepting always closes any open contribution request for that problem.</p>
        <p>Accepting or closing returns you to the contributions list, with a banner summarising what was applied (e.g. "line added to Topo #74", with the topo linked) and how many contributions are still pending. Updates that don't change the state stay on the edit page.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Unlisted problems</p>
        <p>Submissions for a problem that isn't on the map yet arrive with a name/URL but no linked problem. The edit page shows a <strong>Create &amp; link problem</strong> form — pick the area and set name/grade/steepness to create a real problem (it inherits the submitted GPS), then accept to import the photo and line.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Bulk close</p>
        <p>On the list, tick rows and use <strong>Close selected</strong> to decline several at once, with an optional shared note for the decline emails. The edit page also records when each contribution was accepted/closed and by whom.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Open data export</p>
        <p><strong>Currently development-only</strong> — disabled in production until the other contributors have agreed to the CC0 license. In development, the problems dataset is downloadable as CSV from <code>/data/problems.csv</code> (linked from the About page and footer). It includes <code>id, name, grade, steepness, latitude, longitude, area, url</code> for problems in <strong>published areas with a location</strong> — unpublished areas, unlocated problems and contributor details are never exported.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Email delivery</p>
        <p>Contributor emails are skipped when the contribution has no email address (the field is optional on the public form). In <strong>development</strong> nothing is actually sent — emails are written to <code>tmp/mails/</code> for inspection. In <strong>production</strong>, sending uses the SMTP server configured under the <code>smtp</code> key in Rails credentials (address, port, username, password, from). See the "Email Setup" section of <code>docs/admin_guide.md</code> for the full checklist and how to test from the console.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Offline Use %>
    <section id="offline-use">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Offline Use (Map & Photos)" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Mobile signal often drops out on the moor, so every area page has a <strong>"Save map &amp; photos for offline"</strong> button (under the area description). One tap makes the whole area usable with no connection — both the topo photos and the interactive map.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">What gets saved</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li><strong>Topo photos</strong> for the area</li>
          <li><strong>Problem pages</strong> for the area — tap a problem on the map offline and its page (topo + route line) opens</li>
          <li><strong>The base map</strong> for the area's bounding box — map tiles (zoom 0–14, over-zoomed for the close-up view), style, glyphs and sprites</li>
          <li><strong>Overlay data</strong> — the problem/boulder and area-label data shown on the map</li>
          <li><strong>The app shell</strong> — the area and map pages plus the JS/CSS (incl. the MapLibre map library) so the map renders with no network</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A single area is only a few MB. After saving, a timestamp and a <strong>Remove</strong> link appear; Remove clears that area's tiles, photos and pages. If some files fail to download, the saved message says how many — re-save on a better connection.</p>
        <p><strong>Checking a saved area:</strong> the <a href="/en/offline-status" class="text-emerald-700 underline">offline status page</a> ("Details" next to a saved area) shows the service worker version, cache contents, the pinned tile snapshot, and whether each saved area is complete. If offline mode misbehaves in the field, a screenshot of that page is the bug report.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Implementation: a service worker (<code>public/service-worker.js</code>) serves cached assets offline — it also handles Turbo Drive link clicks (not just full navigations) and races the network against a 3.5&nbsp;s timeout so a weak signal falls back to cache quickly. <code>offline_download_controller.js</code> pre-fetches everything using the area's bounds/URLs from <code>Areas::OfflineDataController</code>, counting failures, and re-pins a <em>fresh</em> OpenFreeMap style + TileJSON on every save (their tile URLs contain a rotating dated snapshot — downloading against a stale one yields an empty map). End-to-end check: <code>script/offline_check.mjs</code>. <strong>Known limitation:</strong> problems in a <em>different</em>, un-downloaded area still need a connection.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</div>
